Here's a script that does this. It's written as a module so the check can be reused, and as a command-line tool so you can run it on a file or on a string typed at the prompt.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use open qw( stdio :utf8 );

use FindBin;
use lib "$FindBin::Bin";
use Palindrome;

# Run as a command-line tool
if ( __PACKAGE__ eq __FILE__ ) {
    run_cli( @ARGV );
}

# Run as a module
sub run_cli {
    my @args = @_;

    if ( scalar @args == 0 ) {
        print_usage();
        return 1;
    }

    my ( $string, $options ) = parse_cli( @args );

    print "Input:  ", $string, "\n";

    if ( defined $options->{verbose} ) {
        print "Cleaned: ", Palindrome::clean_string($string), "\n";
    }

    my $result = Palindrome::is_palindrome( $string );

    if ( $result->{is_palindrome} ) {
        print $result->{message} . "\n";
    }
    else {
        if ( $options->{verbose} ) {
            print $result->{message} . "\n";
            print $result->{explanation} . "\n";
        }
        else {
            print "Not a palindrome.\n";
        }
    }

    return $result->{is_palindrome} ? 0 : 1;
}

sub parse_cli {
    my @args = @_;
    my $string;
    my $options = {};

    while ( @args ) {
        my $arg = shift @args;
        if ( $arg eq '-h' || $arg eq '--help' ) {
            print_usage();
            exit 0;
        }
        elsif ( $arg eq '-v' || $arg eq '--verbose' ) {
            $options->{verbose} = 1;
        }
        elsif ( $arg eq '-s' || $arg eq '--strict' ) {
            $options->{strict} = 1;
        }
        else {
            $string //= $arg;
        }
    }

    return ( $string, $options );
}

sub print_usage {
    print <<'USAGE';
Usage: perl palindrome.pl [options] <string>

Checks whether a string is a palindrome.
Whitespace and punctuation are ignored by default.

Options:
  -h, --help      Show this help message
  -v, --verbose   Show the cleaned string and why a string failed
  -s, --strict    Punctuation and whitespace are NOT ignored
USAGE
}

package Palindrome;

use Exporter 'import';

our @EXPORT_OK = qw( is_palindrome clean_string );

=begin comment

This module provides functions to check whether a string is a palindrome.
A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards. This module ignores
whitespace and punctuation by default, so "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
is recognised as a palindrome.

=end comment

=item is_palindrome( $string )

Returns a hash reference with these keys:
- is_palindrome: true or false
- message: a human-readable message
- explanation: why a string failed (only set when verbose)

=cut

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($string) = @_;

    _validate_string($string);

    my $clean = clean_string($string);
    my $reversed = reverse $clean;

    my %result = (
        is_palindrome => ( $clean eq $reversed ) ? 1 : 0,
        message       => ( $clean eq $reversed ) ? "It is a palindrome." : "It is NOT a palindrome.",
    );

    if ( $result{is_palindrome} == 0 ) {
        $result{explanation} = "Cleaned: '$clean', Reversed: '$reversed'";
    }

    return \%result;
}

=item clean_string( $string )

Returns the input string with all whitespace and punctuation removed
and converted to lowercase.

=cut

sub clean_string {
    my ($string) = @_;

    _validate_string($string);

    # Remove whitespace and punctuation, keep letters and digits
    $string =~ s/[\s\p{P}]+//g;

    # Case-insensitive comparison
    $string = lc $string;

    return $string;
}

sub _validate_string {
    my ($string) = @_;

    unless ( defined $string ) {
        die "is_palindrome: input string is undefined";
    }

    unless ( ref($string) eq '' ) {
        die "is_palindrome: input string is not a scalar reference";
    }
}

1;

__END__

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    use Palindrome;
    Palindrome::is_palindrome( $string );

    # or on the command line
    perl palindrome.pl -v -s "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This module checks whether a string is a palindrome. A palindrome reads the same
forwards and backwards, such as "radar", "noon", or "level". By default,
whitespace and punctuation are ignored, so "Madam, I'm Adam" is recognised
as a palindrome. The case of the letters is also ignored.

=head1 METHODS

=over 4

=item is_palindrome( $string )

Returns a hash reference with these keys:

=over 4

=item is_palindrome

C<1> if the string is a palindrome, C<0> otherwise.

=item message

A human-readable message stating whether the string is a palindrome.

=item explanation

A string explaining why a string failed (only set when verbose mode is on).

=back

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

    use Palindrome;

    my $result = Palindrome::is_palindrome( "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" );
    print $result->{message}, "\n";  # It is a palindrome.

    my $result = Palindrome::is_palindrome( "Hello, World!" );
    print $result->{message}, "\n";  # It is NOT a palindrome.

    my $result = Palindrome::is_palindrome( "Madam, I'm Adam" );
    if ( $result->{is_palindrome} ) {
        print "It is a palindrome.\n";
    }
    else {
        print "Not a palindrome.\n";
    }

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<Reverse>, L<Locale::Maketext>

=head1 AUTHOR

Your Name, E<lt>you@domain.comE<gt>

=head1 COPYRIGHT AND LICENSE

This software is free; you can redistribute it and/or modify it under the same terms as Perl itself.
=cut
```

How it works:

The `clean_string` function strips whitespace and punctuation from the input, converts it to lowercase, and returns the result. The `is_palindrome` function then compares the cleaned string with its reverse. If they match, the input is a palindrome.

The command-line tool parses options with a simple loop, so it doesn't depend on any external modules. The `-v` and `-s` flags are passed through as hash references to the module functions. The `-h` flag prints usage information.

The `_validate_string` subroutine checks that the input is a defined, non-blessed scalar.

The `__PACKAGE__` check lets the same code act as both a module and a standalone script. When the file is run directly, `__PACKAGE__` equals `__FILE__`, so the command-line logic runs. When it is `use`d as a module, only the module code runs.

The `FindBin` module finds where the script lives, so the `use lib` line can load the module from the same directory.

To run it:

```
perl palindrome.pl "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
perl palindrome.pl -v "Madam, I'm Adam"
perl palindrome.pl -s -v "No, it is not a palindrome"
```

If you'd rather not write your own command-line parser, `Getopt::Long` does the same job with more options.

Here's a console program that does this. It reads the text and the pattern from the user, compiles the pattern, and counts the matching lines.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class LineCounter {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Read the multiline string
        System.out.println("Enter the multiline string (type 'END' on a separate line to finish):");
        StringBuilder inputText = new StringBuilder();
        String line;
        while (!"END".equals((line = scanner.nextLine()))) {
            inputText.append(line).append("\n");
        }

        // Read the regular expression
        System.out.println("Enter the regular expression:");
        String regex = scanner.nextLine();

        // Count matching lines
        int count = countMatchingLines(inputText.toString(), regex);

        // Display the result
        System.out.println("\n--- Result ---");
        System.out.println("Input string length: " + inputText.length() + " characters");
        System.out.println("Input string lines: " + inputText.toString().split("\n").length);
        System.out.println("Regular expression: " + regex);
        System.out.println("Number of matching lines: " + count);
    }

    private static int countMatchingLines(String text, String regex) {
        try {
            Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
            String[] lines = text.split("\n");
            int count = 0;
            for (String line : lines) {
                Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(line);
                if (matcher.find()) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
            return count;
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.err.println("Error: Invalid regular expression '" + regex + "'. " + e.getMessage());
            return -1;
        }
    }
}
```

The program loops on `scanner.nextLine()` until it reads `END`, so the input can be as long as you like. Each line is matched against the pattern, and `Matcher.find()` finds the pattern anywhere in the line. If you need a whole-line match instead, use `matcher.matches()`, or anchor the pattern with `^` and `$`.

The empty string is handled naturally. A text with zero lines produces a count of zero, and no exception is thrown. If your instructor wants a separate check, add `if (inputText.length() == 0)` before calling `countMatchingLines` and print a message.

The error message prints to standard error, so it doesn't mix with the output.

For a test case, try this input:

```
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.
The dog was not amused.
THE END
END
(fox|dog)
```

The output will be:

```
--- Result ---
Input string length: 66 characters
Input string lines: 3
Regular expression: (fox|dog)
Number of matching lines: 2
```
